**Lemma 26.3 (credited descriptors with full-family witnesses).**
The descriptor image on $\mathcal F_D$ consists of all $2n+1$ values
$B$ and $(j,e)$. For every actual source, a prefix Read at $p_i$ has
reply

$$
r_t(p_i)=
\begin{cases}
\mathsf{absent},&\partial_p(t)=(j,e),\ j<i,\\
\ell_e,&\partial_p(t)=(i,e),\\
\mathsf{branch},&\partial_p(t)=(j,e),\ j>i,\text{ or }\partial_p(t)=B.
\end{cases}
\tag{GDB.106}
$$

Proof. The actual witnesses are
$C_{p_j}[\ell_e]$ and $C_p[\langle\alpha,\alpha\rangle]$.
Because their paths start with $R$, their outer left child is alpha.
Their right-subtree heights are respectively $j-1$ and $n$, so they
belong to $\mathcal F_D$ even when $D=n$. Each has its indicated
first leaf or branch endpoint. On an arbitrary source, every strict
ancestor of its first path leaf is branch, that leaf has its actual
label, and every strict extension of it is absent. If the endpoint is
branch, every prefix is branch. This proves (GDB.106).
These are [AAR, Definition 13.6 and Lemma 13.7][AAR]'s actual witnesses
and prefix-reply law, reused on their original bounded family. $\square$

